Install a treerig binary as the teardown pin, by downloading a release (`pin
--release X.Y.Z` or `pin --latest`) or from a file (`pin --file PATH --sha256
HEX --release LABEL`). With --release X.Y.Z, or --latest for the newest release
that is not a pre-release, treerig downloads that release from GitHub, has
cosign (3.0.0 or newer, found on your PATH) verify the release's checksums.txt
against the release workflow's signature, checks the archive for this platform
against it, and pins the `treerig` inside. Nothing is pinned until you approve
the plan. With --file PATH, treerig pins a local binary you have verified
yourself: you give its sha256, and this approves exactly the bytes you hold.
Take the digest of the binary itself (for a download, extract `treerig` from the
archive you verified, then run `sha256sum treerig`); it is a meaningful check
only when it comes from a second trusted source, such as your own build. The
file is read once, those bytes are hashed and compared with it, and the same
bytes are stored in the control home. With --file, no signature is checked and
cosign is not run; --release is then a label for the pin. Nothing is run from
the pinned binary. There is one pin per control home, and pinning again replaces
it, or adds this platform to it when it is the same release. The command shows
what it will pin and asks you to type a phrase, unless you pass --yes.

Usage:
  treerig teardown pin (--release X.Y.Z | --latest | --file PATH) [flags]

Flags:
      --file string      pin this treerig binary instead of downloading a
                         release; needs --sha256 and --release
  -h, --help             help for pin
      --latest           download the newest release that is not a pre-release
      --release string   the release to download and verify, such as 1.2.3 or
                         0.1.0-rc.1 (no leading v); with --file, a label for the
                         pin instead
      --sha256 string    with --file: the sha256 of that file itself, from a
                         source you trust
      --yes              skip the confirmation phrase

Global Flags:
      --config string   path to treerig.toml (default: nearest above cwd)
  -V, --version         output the version number
